---
import MainLayout from '../layouts/MainLayout.astro';
---

<MainLayout title="Value Framework - Dan McSpirit">
  <div id="shell">
    <!-- IDENTITY COLUMN -->
    <aside class="col col-identity">
      <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: baseline; margin-bottom: 30px;">
        <div class="name">Dan McSpirit</div>
        <div class="metadata">Denver, Colorado</div>
      </div>

      <h1 style="margin-bottom: 26px;">
        Value Framework
      </h1>

      <p style="margin-bottom: 28px;">
        How capital allocation determines whether value gets created or destroyed. The principles that guide my work as an investor, builder, and teacher.
      </p>

      <div class="divider"></div>

      <div style="height: 46px;"></div>
      <div class="scrollfade"></div>
    </aside>

    <!-- WORK COLUMN -->
    <section class="col col-work">
      <div class="pad" style="padding-top: var(--sp-3xl);">
        <!-- Price vs Value -->
        <div style="margin-bottom: 48px;">
          <div class="section-label" style="margin-bottom: 16px;">Price vs Value</div>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            Price is what you pay. Value is what you get. The gap between them is where opportunity and risk live.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            Most market participants confuse the two. A stock trading at $50 is not worth $50. Its price is $50. What you own—its cash generation, growth trajectory, competitive position, balance sheet strength—determines its value. When price and value diverge, you have either opportunity or danger.
          </p>
          <p style="color: var(--faint); font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;">
            The discipline is in the work: building a model that captures what the business actually does, testing it against reality, and having the conviction to act when price and value are clearly misaligned.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Capital Allocation -->
        <div style="margin-bottom: 48px;">
          <div class="section-label" style="margin-bottom: 16px;">Capital Allocation</div>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            Every decision about where capital goes is a decision about value creation or destruction. This is true whether you're managing a company, a portfolio, or a career.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            A company that deploys capital into projects with returns above its cost of capital creates value. One that deploys below its cost of capital destroys it, regardless of how well it executes. Management's primary job is capital allocation.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            The same logic applies to your own capital—financial and human. Where you invest your attention, time, and money determines whether you create value for yourself and others.
          </p>
          <p style="color: var(--faint); font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;">
            This is why I focus on companies and projects where capital has been systematically misallocated. The work is identifying what's being done wrong and what would create value if done differently.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Leverage -->
        <div style="margin-bottom: 48px;">
          <div class="section-label" style="margin-bottom: 16px;">Leverage</div>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            Leverage amplifies returns. It also amplifies risk. The mistake is treating it as separate from risk—it is risk.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            A company with no leverage might return 8% on equity. With 2x leverage, it might return 15%. But it's also one down quarter away from covenant violations and dilutive equity raises. The higher return comes with a tail risk that most investors price poorly.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            Leverage works beautifully in good times and devastates in bad ones. The companies that survive and prosper through cycles are those that use leverage only when they can sustain it through the worst scenarios they can imagine.
          </p>
          <p style="color: var(--faint); font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;">
            When analyzing leverage, I work backward from: what would break this? Not what might break it. What would actually break it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Risk vs Return -->
        <div style="margin-bottom: 48px;">
          <div class="section-label" style="margin-bottom: 16px;">Risk vs Return</div>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            Risk and return are inextricably linked. You cannot have high return without high risk. What you can have is high risk with low return—that's just bad investing.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            The hard part is identifying which risks are actually priced into the return you're being offered. A stock trading at 8x earnings in a stable, high-return business is lower risk than one trading at 20x earnings in a commodity business, regardless of what the volatility charts say.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            If you can't see the risk, you haven't looked hard enough. This is where most analysis fails. It's easy to model the base case. It's harder—and more important—to identify what could go wrong and whether you're being paid for that possibility.
          </p>
          <p style="color: var(--faint); font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;">
            The returns go to those who can see risks others miss and price them correctly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Verification -->
        <div style="margin-bottom: 48px;">
          <div class="section-label" style="margin-bottom: 16px;">Verification</div>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            Your model is not the business. Your model is how you think about the business. The gap between the two is where mistakes happen.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            Verification means stress-testing your model against reality. Running the numbers against what actually happened. Finding the places where your thinking was wrong. A model that passes verification is one you can trust. One that doesn't is just a story you told yourself.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            The best verification is simple: can you predict what happens next? If your model is right, you should be able to forecast results, and they should match. If they don't, your model is wrong.
          </p>
          <p style="color: var(--faint); font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;">
            This is why I build regression harnesses and run second systems with no stake in the first one's conclusions. You need an independent check.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Teaching -->
        <div style="margin-bottom: 48px;">
          <div class="section-label" style="margin-bottom: 16px;">Teaching</div>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            Teaching forces you to think clearly. If you can't explain something simply, you don't understand it well enough.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
            I teach because it sharpens my thinking and because the gap between where most students are and where they could be—in finance, in analysis, in how they think about risk and return—is massive. The work is closing that gap.
          </p>
          <p style="color: var(--faint); font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;">
            A student who understands these principles and can apply them has an enormous advantage. Most never learn them at all.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="scrollfade"></div>
    </section>

    <!-- PROJECTS COLUMN -->
    <section class="col col-projects">
      <div class="pad" style="padding-top: var(--sp-3xl);">
        <div class="section-label muted" style="margin-bottom: 24px;">How this works</div>

        <div style="background: #fbfaf6; border: 1px solid var(--rule-light); border-radius: 4px; padding: 24px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
          <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;">
            These principles aren't theoretical. They're applied in every valuation I build, every company I analyze, every investment I make. When they're followed, value gets created. When they're violated, it gets destroyed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="margin-bottom: 40px;">
          <div class="section-label muted" style="margin-bottom: 12px;">In practice</div>
          <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink);">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">Every model starts with: what is the business actually doing?</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">Then: what would break this? What's the tail risk?</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">Then: is the price I'm paying justified by what I know and what could go wrong?</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">Finally: can I verify this? Can I test it against reality?</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="divider"></div>

        <div style="margin-top: 40px;">
          <div class="section-label muted" style="margin-bottom: 12px;">The hard truth</div>
          <p style="font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--faint); margin: 0;">
            Finance's golden rule: You can't get something for nothing. Risk and return are linked. Capital gets allocated based on how people think about the future. Value gets created or destroyed based on whether those thoughts match reality.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="scrollfade"></div>
    </section>
  </div>
</MainLayout>

<style>
  .divider {
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--rule);
    margin: 40px 0;
  }
</style>
